{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788297787926,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260973ffeOIaNGhvLCc80qv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-abd3942caa09f05b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/plans/questions.md","offset":525,"limit":70},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/plans/questions.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n525: calling a private helper.\n526: \n527: None of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\n528: written codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\n529: exactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\n530: internal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\n531: \n532: ### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\n533: \n534: The representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\n535: JavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\n536: agreement harness, which checks bytes.\n537: \n538: - **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\n539:   do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\n540:   points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\n541:   ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n542:   this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\n543:   [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md).\n544: - **NaN and Infinity.** IEEE produces both; JSON can spell neither. A language whose values\n545:   are JSON-shaped either forbids them (a check on every producing operation), maps them to\n546:   something at the boundary (jq-style lossiness, the kind this design usually refuses), or\n547:   admits values its own output cannot carry.\n548: - **Division by zero.** The Int rule says a zero divisor is the only way arithmetic fails.\n549:   IEEE says `1.0 / 0.0` is `Infinity`, no failure at all. Keeping both means division's\n550:   behavior depends on its operand type; unifying means overriding one standard or the other.\n551: \n552: None of this blocks anything else, so it waits for `Float` to be forced by a real program the\n553: way `inputs` and `jsonlines` were.\n\n(End of file - total 553 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"calling a private helper.\n\nNone of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\nwritten codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\nexactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\ninternal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\n\n### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\n\nThe representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\nJavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\nagreement harness, which checks bytes.\n\n- **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\n  do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\n  points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\n  ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n  this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\n  [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md).\n- **NaN and Infinity.** IEEE produces both; JSON can spell neither. A language whose values","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149/plans/questions.md","text":"calling a private helper.\n\nNone of these were needed to get one function (`unlines`) out of six backends' worth of hand-\nwritten codegen, and building them speculatively risks shaping them around a prelude that has\nexactly one function in it. What would force an answer: a second prelude function needing an\ninternal helper, at which point the non-`pub`-is-simply-absent rule stops being free.\n\n### Q37. How do floats print, and what are NaN and Infinity in a JSON-shaped value model?\n\nThe representation is [decided](../draft.md#decided-float-is-javascripts-double): IEEE 754 binary64,\nJavaScript's number. Everything observable about it is not, and each piece has to survive the\nagreement harness, which checks bytes.\n\n- **Printing.** Every backend must render the same double to the same text, and their defaults\n  do not agree on shortest-roundtrip versus fixed formatting, or on `1e21`-style switchover\n  points. The printer is currently the only specification of output format\n  ([Q35](#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)), which makes\n  this a per-backend conformance rule to be stated by hand, the same lesson as\n  [backends can agree and still be wrong](../research-log/backends-can-agree-and-still-be-wrong.md).\n- **NaN and Infinity.** IEEE produces both; JSON can spell neither. A language whose values\n  are JSON-shaped either forbids them (a check on every producing operation), maps them to\n  something at the boundary (jq-style lossiness, the kind this design usually refuses), or\n  admits values its own output cannot carry.\n- **Division by zero.** The Int rule says a zero divisor is the only way arithmetic fails.\n  IEEE says `1.0 / 0.0` is `Infinity`, no failure at all. Keeping both means division's\n  behavior depends on its operand type; unifying means overriding one standard or the other.\n\nNone of this blocks anything else, so it waits for `Float` to be forced by a real program the\nway `inputs` and `jsonlines` were.","lineStart":525,"lineEnd":553,"totalLines":553,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/questions.md","time":{"start":1788297787911,"end":1788297787924}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The branch is behind origin/main by 116 commits. This is a concern. The `--all` log shows a lot of work on main that this worktree's branch doesn't have. But the brief explicitly says \"work ONLY inside this worktree; do NOT push\". \n\nHmm, but the current branch `issue-149` is at commit 13d4098 which is behind origin/main by 116 commits. This is a significant divergence. Since this is a fresh delegated worker lane for implementing Float, I should work on the current state of the branch.\n\nWait - but there might be an issue. If the branch is 116 commits behind main, the float implementation might conflict with main. But my brief is clear: work only in this worktree, don't push, commit on this branch. So I'll implement against the current state.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Let me check what commit the branch is at and what the relationship is. The branch issue-149 is at 13d4098. That's the \"Board: file gh:148...\" commit. This is a fresh lane for implementing issue 149 (Float). So the branch is the lane. Being behind main by 116 commits is just because the lane was created from an older point. I shouldn't pull because the brief says work only in this worktree and don't push. But pulling would be fine locally... but the brief doesn't ask me to. I'll work against current state.\n\nLet me read the Q37 question fully to understand the printing decision.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05edab261001ybDAoz2g9ZE210","sessionID":"ses_fa1260973ffeOIaNGhvLCc80qv","messageID":"msg_05eda7f63001gCYn7z2tirvZ1O"}}
