{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788298005454,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260973ffeOIaNGhvLCc80qv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"todowrite","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-85a8aa62f5ba3bda","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"todos":[{"content":"Explore codebase: how Int is handled across parse/check/tags/emit backends","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Decide float printing format (ESCALATION if needed)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Add Type::Float to ty.rs","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Lexer/parser: parse float literals","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Checker: float literal + arithmetic type rules","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"tags.rs + corpus case slice; commit","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Backend: Lua","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Backend: JS","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Backend: Native (LLVM)","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Backend: jq","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Backend: Go","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Backend: Python","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Backend: Rust","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Full just check green + fmt + clippy","status":"pending","priority":"high"}]},"output":"[\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Explore codebase: how Int is handled across parse/check/tags/emit backends\",\n    \"status\": \"in_progress\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Decide float printing format (ESCALATION if needed)\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Add Type::Float to ty.rs\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Lexer/parser: parse float literals\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Checker: float literal + arithmetic type rules\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"tags.rs + corpus case slice; commit\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Backend: Lua\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Backend: JS\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Backend: Native (LLVM)\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Backend: jq\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Backend: Go\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Backend: Python\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Backend: Rust\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Full just check green + fmt + clippy\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  }\n]","metadata":{"todos":[{"content":"Explore codebase: how Int is handled across parse/check/tags/emit backends","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Decide float printing format (ESCALATION if needed)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Add Type::Float to ty.rs","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Lexer/parser: parse float literals","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Checker: float literal + arithmetic type rules","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"tags.rs + corpus case slice; commit","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Backend: Lua","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Backend: JS","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Backend: Native (LLVM)","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Backend: jq","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Backend: Go","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Backend: Python","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Backend: Rust","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Full just check green + fmt + clippy","status":"pending","priority":"high"}],"truncated":false},"title":"14 todos","time":{"start":1788298005445,"end":1788298005450}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This is a big task. Let me plan carefully. The 7 backends: Lua, Js, Native (LLVM), Jq, Go, Py, Rust.\n\nLet me look at how Int is handled in each file. This is going to be a lot of code. Let me be systematic and use the explore agent to gather the details of each backend's Int handling, then implement Float analogously.\n\nActually, given the scale and that I need to make careful edits, let me do focused reads. Let me start with the core: parse.rs, ast.rs, tir.rs, check, tags.rs, then each emit backend.\n\nLet me set up the todo list first.\n\nLet me think about the printing format decision. This is the \"needs a call\" item. Given ADR says Float is JavaScript's number, the most principled choice is to match ECMAScript's Number::toString. But that's complex. \n\nActually, let me reconsider. A cleaner decision that all 7 backends can satisfy: define float printing as the **shortest round-trip decimal, in JavaScript's `String(n)` style**: \n- Integral values print without decimal point (1.0 -> \"1\")\n- Uses scientific notation following ECMAScript rules (|x| >= 1e21 or < 1e-6)\n\nBut implementing ECMAScript's exact exponent formatting (like \"1e+21\", \"1e-7\") in Go, Lua, Python, Rust, jq, LLVM C is painful.\n\nAlternatively, the simplest portable decision: **match Rust's `format!(\"{}\", f)`** (shortest round-trip, e.g. 1.0 -> \"1\", 1e21 -> \"1000000000000000000000\"). But Python prints 1.0 as \"1.0\", Go prints 1.0 as \"1\"... they differ on the integral case.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's actually feasible. The cleanest cross-language agreement approach: since the value is known at compile time for literals, but arithmetic produces runtime values, the printing happens at runtime in emitted code.\n\nActually, here's a key insight: I can define the format and implement it in each backend using that backend's own floating formatting PLUS normalization. E.g., define: print the shortest round-trip decimal. If the result has no '.', 'e', 'E', 'n', 'N' character (i.e. it's an integral value with no exponent), then... different backends print integers differently.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the best decision given the constraints is to pick a specific well-defined format and normalize across backends with explicit post-processing in each emitted printer.\n\nThe most reproducible and testable format I can fully control: I'll decide that a float prints as **the shortest decimal that round-trips, using Rust's `{}` formatting rules** (which is exactly ECMAScript-like for most, but not identical). Actually let me check what Rust's `{}` gives for various doubles vs other languages:\n\n- 1.0: Rust \"1\", JS \"1\", Go \"1\", Python repr \"1.0\", Lua \"1.0\", jq \"1\", C \"1\"\n- 0.1: Rust \"0.1\", JS \"0.1\", Go \"0.1\", Python \"0.1\", Lua \"0.1\", jq \"0.1\"\n- 1e21: Rust \"1000000000000000000000\", JS \"1e+21\", Go \"1e+21\", Python \"1e+21\", Lua \"1e+21\"\n- 1e-7: Rust \"0.0000001\", JS \"1e-7\", Go \"1e-07\", Python \"1e-07\"\n\nSo they differ on: integral values (whether \".0\" or not), and exponent formatting.\n\nThe cleanest decision to make all 7 agree: **The float printer emits, for every value, the shortest round-trip decimal. When the value is a mathematical integer that round-trips, print it WITHOUT a decimal point (like 1, 2, 100). Use scientific notation with a specific exponent format whenever |value| >= 1e21 or < 1e-6.** \n\nBut to make backends agree on the actual digits, I'd need each backend to implement this. The digits part (shortest round-trip) they all do natively. The integral case: Rust/Go/JS/jq print integers without \".0\"; Python and Lua print \"1.0\". So I'd need Python/Lua to strip the trailing \".0\". And the exponent case: they differ in exponent digit count (1e-7 vs 1e-07) and \"+\".\n\nThis is getting really messy to implement across all 7.\n\nLet me step back and consider: maybe the simplest robust decision is **scientific notation for everything, always**, in a fully controlled format. E.g., always print like `%g`-style shortest round trip... no.\n\nAlternatively: The decision could be to print floats in a way that guarantees agreement by having each backend convert to a canonical string via a small amount of code. Given all 7 use shortest-roundtrip algorithms, the remaining differences are formatting cosmetics. \n\nI think the pragmatic, defensible decision is: **Use ECMAScript's Number::toString as the spec** (since ADR explicitly says Float IS JavaScript's number). Then implement it consistently. But implementing EC's exact exponent rules in 7 languages is a lot.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. Let me look at how the backends currently format Int, and whether there's a shared runtime. The Native/LLVM backend has RUNTIME_C (a C runtime). That's one shared printer I could control fully. For Lua it's embedded. For the others it's emitted source.\n\nGiven the enormity, let me make a decision that minimizes implementation burden while being principled and testable: \n\n**Decision: A Float prints as the shortest decimal representation that round-trips, formatted exactly like JavaScript's `String(n)`/`n.toString()`, including the integral case (no decimal point) and ECMAScript's exponent formatting.** This is the most defensible because the ADR literally defines Float as JavaScript's number.\n\nBut implementing ECMAScript toString's exponent threshold (>= 1e21 or < 1e-6) and its exponent format (\"e+21\", \"e-7\", no leading zeros) in C (LLVM runtime), Lua, Go, Python, jq, JS, Rust... \n\nHmm, actually JS is trivial (just `String(n)` or `${n}`). jq: jq formats numbers with `tostring` which... jq uses Go-like formatting. Python: `repr(float)` but that gives \"1.0\" for integral. \n\nLet me think about the \"integral case\" decision separately, because it's the biggest divergence. Options:\n(a) Integral floats print WITHOUT decimal point (1.0 -> \"1\") — JS, Go, jq, Rust native.\n(b) Integral floats ALWAYS print WITH \".0\" (1.0 -> \"1.0\") — Python, Lua native.\n\nFor (b), I'd need JS/Go/jq/Rust to append \".0\" when the shortest repr is integral. That's actually easy: detect if the printed string has no '.'/'e' and append \".0\". And Python/Lua already do it.\n\nFor (a), I'd need Python/Lua to strip a trailing \".0\". Also easy-ish: if repr ends with \".0\", strip it.\n\nThen the exponent case. JS uses \"1e+21\"; Python \"1e+21\"; Go \"1e+21\" (but with \"1e-07\" for negative exponent, two digits!). Lua \"1e+21\". jq uses Go. C \"%g\". Rust \"1000000000000000000000\" (no exponent for large, only uses exponent for < 1e-4 maybe... actually Rust switches to exponent for values < 1e-4 and >= 1e16? no). Let me not rely on Rust's format.\n\nThe exponent formats: Go pads exponent to 2 digits (\"1e+21\" has 2-digit? \"21\" is 2 digits so fine, but \"1e-07\" has 2-digit negative with leading zero). Python gives \"1e-07\" too (2-digit). JS gives \"1e-7\" (no leading zero, and \"+\" for positive: \"1e+21\"). jq... Let me check what jq does. jq's number formatting follows its own rules - jq prints 1e21 as \"1e+21\" and 1e-7 as \"1e-07\"? Actually jq prints 1000000000000000000000 as \"1e+21\"? Let me not guess.\n\nThis cross-backend agreement is genuinely hard. Given the \"needs a call during implementation\" framing and the difficulty, I think the right move is to make a clear decision and implement it robustly, testing byte-for-byte via the corpus.\n\nLet me reconsider: what if I define the format as **always scientific-free shortest round-trip with full digits**, i.e., never use exponent notation, just print all digits? E.g., 1e21 -> \"1000000000000000000000\", 1e-7 -> \"0.0000001\". Then:\n- Integral: JS \"1\", Go \"1\", Rust \"1\", jq \"1\" — but Python \"1.0\", Lua \"1.0\". Handle by stripping \".0\".\n- Non-integral with no exponent: JS \"0.1\", Go \"0.1\", Rust \"0.1\", Python \"0.1\", Lua \"0.1\", jq \"0.1\", C \"0.1\" — all agree naturally.\n- Large: JS \"1e+21\" (uses exponent!). Go \"1e+21\". So JS/Go still use exponent for >= 1e21. To force full digits I'd need to override their formatting, which is hard.\n\nUgh. The reality is that JS and Go (and the other C-derived) will auto-switch to scientific at 1e21. So to get byte-for-byte agreement I MUST specify scientific formatting rules and implement them.\n\nOK here's my refined decision. I'll specify: **A Float prints using shortest-round-trip digits with a fixed, explicit scientific-notation policy, matching JavaScript's `String(number)` format exactly** (ECMAScript 2023 Number::toString), because the ADR declares Float is JavaScript's number, and JS is the one backend where the format is literally the language's own default (zero code). I'll implement this same format in the other six.\n\nECMAScript Number::toString(x):\n- If x is NaN -> \"NaN\"\n- If x is +0 -> \"0\" (note: no \"-0\" handling... actually -0 -> \"0\")\n- If x is -0 -> \"0\"\n- If x is +Infinity -> \"Infinity\"\n- If x is -Infinity -> \"-Infinity\"\n- Otherwise let n, k, s be integers such that k >= 1, 10^(k-1) <= s < 10^k, s*10^(n-k) = x, s is not divisible by 10 (k is the number of significant digits, shortest). \n- If k <= n <= 21: digits of s followed by (n-k) zeros -> \"digits\" (integer form)\n- If 0 < n <= 21: digits with decimal point inserted -> \"d.ddd\"\n- If -6 < n <= 0: \"0.\" followed by (-n) zeros then digits -> \"0.000ddd\"\n- Otherwise (n > 21 or n <= -6): scientific -> \"d.ddd e sign exp\" where exp = n-1. Format: first digit, then if k>1 a decimal point and remaining digits, then \"e\", then sign ('+' if positive, '-' if negative), then the exponent as a decimal integer (no leading zeros, no fixed width).\n\nSo JS: 1.0 -> k=1,s=1,n=1 -> 1<=n<=21 -> \"1\". 0.1 -> k=1,s=1,n=0 -> 0<n<=21 false, -6<0<=0 true -> \"0.1\". 1e21 -> n=22 >21 -> scientific \"1e+21\". 1e-7 -> n=-6 -> n<=-6 -> \"1e-7\". 100 -> n=3 -> \"100\". 0.000001 -> n=-5 -> \"0.000001\". \n\nThis is well-defined and JS gets it for free. Now I need to implement this in C (LLVM), Lua, Go, Python, jq, Rust.\n\nImplementing shortest-roundtrip + EC formatting in C/Lua/Go/Python/jq/Rust from scratch is a LOT of work (Ryu algorithm). That's not feasible.\n\nAlternative: use each language's shortest-roundtrip formatting to get the DIGITS, then reformat to EC style. Each language can produce shortest-roundtrip digits (Rust `{}`, Python `repr`, Go `%g`/strconv, Lua `string.format(\"%.17g\")`? no that's not shortest... Lua's default `tostring` gives shortest roundtrip, C needs Ryu but I could use `snprintf(\"%.17g\")` then trim, or use `%.17g` and strip trailing zeros — that gives shortest-ish roundtrip but not necessarily shortest; %.17g is guaranteed roundtrip though).\n\nHmm. Getting shortest-roundtrip is the hard part that Ryu solves. But do I need shortest? I need round-trip (reparsing gives same double) — %.17g guarantees that in C. But the corpus needs byte-for-byte agreement AND round-trip isn't necessarily tested. Actually round-trip isn't strictly required by the acceptance criteria — acceptance is: literals parse, arithmetic works, NaN/Infinity print identically, division by zero returns Infinity. It doesn't explicitly require shortest or round-trip. But good design would want round-trip.\n\nThe corpus tests compare output bytes across 7 backends. So I need the same digits everywhere. If I use shortest-roundtrip (Ryu-like) in each backend, they all produce the same shortest digits (Ryu is deterministic and identical digits across implementations for the same double). Then I apply EC formatting on top. This is the cleanest.\n\nBut implementing shortest-roundtrip (Ryu) in 6 languages is huge. HOWEVER — I don't have to implement Ryu from scratch in each. Most languages already have shortest-roundtrip:\n- Rust: `{}` for f64 — but Rust uses its own exponent rules. I can get shortest digits via `format!(\"{:.17e}\", ...)`? No. Actually Rust `{}` gives shortest roundtrip digits but in decimal-or-scientific form. To extract shortest digits reliably I can format with `{:e}`? Rust's `{:e}` uses 6 sig figs by default, not shortest.\n\nLet me reconsider. This is becoming a rabbit hole. Let me look at what the existing backends already do and how much float infrastructure exists. Maybe there's already a plan or partial float work.\n\nLet me look at the research-log and board for float-related notes, and check whether there's existing float formatting code anywhere.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the decision entirely. The cleanest implementable decision that guarantees byte agreement with minimal per-backend code:\n\n**Decision: A Float value prints as its shortest round-trip decimal representation, formatted exactly as Rust's `f64` `Display` (`format!(\"{}\", f)`), because Rust is the implementation language and its formatting is shortest-roundtrip.** Then I implement THAT format in the other 6.\n\nRust Display rules for f64:\n- Uses shortest round-trip digits\n- 1.0 -> \"1\" (integral, no \".0\")\n- Uses decimal notation for a large range, scientific only for... let me recall: Rust's `{}` for f64: for values with magnitude < 1e-4 (?) uses scientific, else decimal. Actually Rust: `1e-5` -> \"0.00001\"? Let me check. Rust `format!(\"{}\", 1e-5)` = \"0.00001\". `format!(\"{}\", 1e-4)` = \"0.0001\". `format!(\"{}\", 1e21)` = \"1000000000000000000000\". So Rust Display does NOT use scientific at all for these! It uses `1e-5` style? Let me verify... Actually I recall Rust `{}` for f64 uses scientific notation only below 1e-4 (exclusive) — `format!(\"{}\", 1e-5)` = \"0.00001\"? Hmm I'm not 100% sure. Let me just test it in the actual codebase since Rust is available.\n\nSimilarly I'd need to know each language's exact behavior. This is getting complicated.\n\nLet me actually just experiment. Let me write quick checks. But I can't run arbitrary binaries... wait, the denial is on `cargo run -- run some_file.toy` (running the toylang binary). I can still write and run normal Rust/C/Go/Python/etc code for my own investigation? The denial list says \"direct binary execution such as `cargo run -- run some_file.toy` or `./target/debug/toylang ...`\". That's specifically about running the toylang interpreter on .toy files. For investigating float formatting, I can use the languages directly... but actually the brief says \"run scripts as python3 script.py after writing them with the write tool\". So I CAN write a python script and run it. And I can probably run small Rust via... hmm, \"writes under /tmp\" denied, \"multi-command bash lines denied\". I can write scripts in the worktree (untracked, not git-added) and run `python3 script.py`.\n\nBut to investigate Rust f64 Display, I'd need a rust program. That's harder without a scratch project. I can rely on knowledge.\n\nLet me step back and make a robust, well-scoped decision. I'll go with:\n\n**Decision: Float printing follows the ECMAScript Number::toString specification** (the ADR's \"Float is JavaScript's number\" taken literally, and JS is the backend where it's free). For the other six backends, I'll extract the shortest-round-trip significant digits using the shortest-round-trip formatting each already provides natively, then reformat to EC style. \n\nFor getting shortest-roundtrip digits in each backend:\n- **Rust**: `format!(\"{:e}\", f)` — no, 6 digits. Hmm. Rust: to get shortest digits, `format!(\"{}\", f)` gives the shortest representation but in display form. I need to parse it. Actually there's a trick: Rust `{}` produces the shortest round-trip string in \"scientific or decimal\" form. I can normalize. This is doable in the generated Rust since it's Rust.\n- **Go**: `strconv.FormatFloat(f, 'g', -1, 64)` gives shortest roundtrip with %g style (exponent form). I can reformat.\n- **Python**: `repr(f)` gives shortest roundtrip. Reformats.\n- **Lua**: `tostring(f)` or `string.format(\"%.14g\"?...)`. Lua's default `tostring` gives shortest roundtrip (Lua 5.3+). \n- **jq**: jq's `tostring` on a number. jq number formatting — need to check.\n- **C (LLVM runtime)**: I need shortest roundtrip in C. I can implement or use `snprintf(\"%.17g\")` which is NOT shortest but round-trips. That would produce different digits than shortest in some cases (e.g., %.17g of 0.1 = \"0.10000000000000001\"). That breaks agreement. So I'd need to implement shortest-roundtrip in C. That's the Ryu algorithm — significant work but well-documented. Actually, I could implement a simpler \"shortest round-trip via %.17g + trimming\" approach: print with %.17g, then trim trailing zeros after the decimal. Does %.17g + trim = shortest? Not always — shortest round-trip sometimes needs fewer digits than trimming %.17g gives (e.g. for 1e16, %.17g gives \"1.0000000000000000e+16\", trim -> \"1e+16\", that's shortest; but for some values %.17g trimmed may still have more digits than the true shortest). Actually the trimmed %.17g gives the smallest number of digits that round-trips IF we only ever print all 17 and trim trailing zeros — trimming trailing zeros from a round-trip representation still round-trips. But is it the SHORTEST? The shortest round-trip representation might have fewer digits than %.17g-trimmed in the mantissa? E.g., the true shortest for some double might be \"2.147483647\" (10 digits) while %.17g gives \"2.1474836470000000\" trimmed to \"2.147483647\" — same. Actually Ryu's shortest is ≤ 17 digits, and %.17g gives 17 digits which after trimming trailing zeros... but trailing zeros in the middle aren't trimmed. Hmm, Ryu shortest can have fewer than 17 significant digits where %.17g would have a non-zero trailing digit that could be rounded away while still round-tripping. Example: the double 1.0000000000000002e-? ... This is the classic case where 17 digits ≠ shortest. So %.17g-trim is not exactly Ryu.\n\nTo be safe and correct, I'd implement shortest-roundtrip. But implementing Ryu in C for the LLVM runtime... it's a known ~300 line algorithm (the Grisu/Ryu). That's a big chunk.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider — maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me check: do the existing backends already have a shared number-printing mechanism I can hook, or does the corpus even test large/exponent floats? The acceptance criteria: \"NaN/Infinity are producible and print identically across backends\", \"arithmetic (+ - * /) and comparisons work across all seven backends with agreeing output\". Realistic corpus tests will use \"nice\" float values like 3.14, 0.5, 2.0, 1.5, etc., plus NaN/Infinity, plus division results like 1/3 (which gives 0.3333333333333333). Those all have simple shortest-roundtrip digits. The nasty 1e21 case might not even be exercised. But to be robust I should handle it.\n\nGiven the difficulty, let me look at whether there's already any float-related code or a partially-started implementation in this branch, and look at how the LLVM runtime handles numbers (it might already have a float printer or use %g).\n\nLet me look at the LLVM emitter and runtime, and grep for float/NaN/Infinity across src.\n\nLet me also check: is there maybe an existing research note about float printing format decision?\n\nLet me be efficient and gather a lot via grep/explore.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05eddfabe00172zHcpXbd3iXDY","sessionID":"ses_fa1260973ffeOIaNGhvLCc80qv","messageID":"msg_05edbb833001qL6Er9T3q6ftWf"}}
