{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788298486299,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260973ffeOIaNGhvLCc80qv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-bb340aa53e0f2ad2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Explore Int handling across backends","prompt":"I'm implementing a new `Float` type (IEEE binary64) in the toylang language compiler at /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-149. It will mirror how `Int` is handled everywhere. I need a comprehensive map of every place `Int` appears so I can add `Float` analogously.\n\nPlease do a VERY THOROUGH exploration and report back, with file:line references, the following:\n\n1. **src/ast.rs**: How are number literals represented in the AST? Is there a token/expr for int literals? What fields?\n\n2. **src/parse.rs**: How are integer literals lexed/parsed? Where is the number scanning logic? How does the type grammar (`parse_type` or similar) recognize \"Int\"? \n\n3. **src/tir.rs**: How is the typed IR (TIR) structured? Where do literal values live? How is `Int` represented as a value (an enum variant like `TirValue::Int`)? What about the `Kind` enum in tir.rs that maps to tags.rs?\n\n4. **src/check/mod.rs** and **src/check/types.rs**: Where does the checker assign types to int literals? Where are the arithmetic operator rules (+ - * / %) defined and how do they check Int operands? Where are comparison operators checked? Where does the checker compute literal types?\n\n5. **src/check/linearity.rs**: does Int appear?\n\n6. **src/tags.rs**: How are AST/TIR node types tagged? Is there a tag for int literals and arithmetic?\n\n7. **src/emit_*.rs** (emit_go, emit_jq, emit_js, emit_llvm, emit_lua, emit_py, emit_rs, emit_toylang): For EACH backend, how is an Int literal emitted? How is Int arithmetic (+ - * / %) emitted? How is Int comparison emitted? How does each backend print/format an Int value in its runtime/printer function? Report the specific functions and line numbers.\n\n8. **src/emit_llvm.rs**: How does the LLVM backend represent Int (i64?) in its IR? Is there a RUNTIME_C string? How are Int values printed there?\n\n9. **src/prelude.rs**: does Int appear in prelude signatures?\n\n10. **src/tir.rs fusion**: does Int appear?\n\n11. **src/input.rs**: how does input validation handle Int values (serde_json)? \n\n12. **tests/**: Point me to a representative corpus case for Int arithmetic (e.g. tests/corpus/arith_add.yaml), and how tests/corpus.rs runs cases across backends. Also look at tests/step_1.rs or similar for how type errors are snapshot-tested, and tests/int64.rs as an example of a recent numeric-type addition and what it tests.\n\nReport the structure clearly organized by file. Be comprehensive — I need to mirror Int for Float across every file. Do not write any code, just report findings with line numbers.","subagent_type":"explore"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_fa121c1e5ffeGR4RxLtHYkn0VL\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\nI now have a comprehensive picture. Here is the full map of every place `Int` appears, organized by your questions, so you can mirror it for `Float`.\n\nA high-level note first: the codebase already shows you the *second*-numeric-type pattern — `Int64` (kantord/toylang#83) was bolted onto `Int` a while ago and is woven through every file below. `Float` is effectively a third numeric type, and nearly every `Type::Int | Type::Int64` pair below is a spot you must decide whether `Float` joins. The cleanest model to copy is the `Int`/`Int64` split: a literal whose type comes from its position, an arithmetic operator that refuses to mix widths, and a per-backend runtime width.\n\n---\n\n## 1. `src/ast.rs` — number literals in the AST\n\n- **`Expr::Int { value: i64, span: Span }`** — `src/ast.rs:198-201`. The only numeric literal node. Note it stores a plain `i64`; the *type* of the literal is not in the AST (that's decided by the checker, see §4).\n- `Expr::span()` match arm for `Expr::Int` — `src/ast.rs:367`.\n- The type grammar is separate: **`TypeExpr::Named { name: String, args, span }`** — `src/ast.rs:88-94`. \"Float\" would be spelled as a plain `Named` type, resolved later by `Type::from_name` (§4/types.rs). No AST change needed for the *type* side.\n- `BinOp` enum (`Add/Sub/Mul/Div/Rem/Eq/Ne/Lt/Le/Gt/Ge`) — `src/ast.rs:17-30`; `is_comparison()` — `33-38`; `is_arithmetic()` — `42-44` (note it deliberately *excludes* `Add`, because `+` also concatenates — see `plus()` in §4).\n\nFor Float you'd add `Expr::Float { value: f64, span }` (or similar) and the matching `span()` arm.\n\n---\n\n## 2. `src/parse.rs` — lexing/parsing numbers and the type grammar\n\nLexer token:\n- **`Tok::Int(i64)`** — `src/parse.rs:39`. Display impl — `84`.\n- Number scanning is in **`read_tok`**, the `'0'..='9'` arm — `src/parse.rs:158-169`: scans ASCII digits, parses to `i64`, errors \"integer ... is out of range\" on overflow. There is **no decimal/exponent handling** — a `.` or `e` is not part of a number literal today.\n- `-` is not folded into literals: it lexes as `Tok::Minus` (comment at `194-195`; arm at `196-204`). Negative literals are `Expr::Neg` over an `Expr::Int` (§4 folds them).\n- Token → AST: **`atom()`** — `src/parse.rs:1210-1271`; the `Tok::Int(value)` arm producing `Expr::Int` is at `src/parse.rs:1214`. Also `Tok::Int` appears in `ident_expr`'s bare-argument check at `src/parse.rs:1168`.\n- **Type grammar**: `type_expr()` — `src/parse.rs:597-640`. Any `Tok::Ident` becomes `TypeExpr::Named` (with optional `<...>` args); it does not special-case built-in scalar names — so \"Float\" as a type name parses with no parser change. Only `Vec`/`Stream` get dedicated nodes (`625-638`, via `ty::takes_type_arg`).\n\nFor Float you need: a new token (e.g. `Tok::Float`), a lexer arm that also accepts `.` and exponent, and an `atom()` arm.\n\n---\n\n## 3. `src/tir.rs` — the typed IR and `Kind`\n\n- **`Kind::Int(i64)`** — `src/tir.rs:29`. Every literal value in the TIR is a `Kind` variant; a Float literal would be `Kind::Float(f64)` (or similar).\n- `Tir { ty: Type, kind: Kind }` — `src/tir.rs:13-16`. Each node carries its checked `Type`.\n- `Kind::Arith { op, lhs, rhs }` — `src/tir.rs:77-81`; doc comment at `73-76` explains wrapping at the node's own width (32-bit for `Int`, 64 for `Int64`).\n- `Kind::Compare { op, lhs, rhs }` — `src/tir.rs:89-93`.\n- `each_node` walker matches `Kind::Int` in its leaf arm — `src/tir.rs:490`.\n- The fusion `flatten` walker — `src/tir.rs:326-389` — has `Kind::Int` only inside the trailing `unreachable!` arm (`_ => unreachable!`), so Float needs nothing there.\n\n---\n\n## 4. `src/check/mod.rs` and `src/check/types.rs` — the checker\n\n`check/types.rs` (type resolution):\n- **`Type::from_name`** — `src/types.rs`→ actually `src/ty.rs:95-104` (the name→`Type` table). This is where `\"Float\"` must be added for `resolve_named` to accept it. `resolve_named` calls it at `src/check/types.rs:324-329`.\n- `RESERVED_TYPE_NAMES` — `src/ty.rs:80` (only `Vec`/`Stream`; built-ins come from `from_name`).\n\n`check/mod.rs` (expression checking):\n- **Literal type assignment** — `Expr::Int` in `synth()`: `src/check/mod.rs:1279-1296`. Rejects a literal over `i32::MAX` unless an `Int64` is expected (the position-based rule). A Float literal would synthesize to `Type::Float` here.\n- **Position-based literal resolution** — `int64_resolved()` — `src/check/mod.rs:2012-2038`: a literal (or `-literal`) met by an `Int64` expectation resolves at 64 bits. This is the model for \"a literal where a Float is expected\" (for Float, since there's one width, this may be trivial or unnecessary). Called from `expect_inner` at `src/check/mod.rs:2634`.\n- **Negative-literal folding** — `Expr::Neg` arm in `synth()`: `src/check/mod.rs:1474-1515`. Folds `-literal` into the literal; otherwise lowers to `0 - x` as `Kind::Arith`. For Float, the same fold applies (though f64 negation has no overflow edge).\n- **Arithmetic operator rules** — `binary()`: `src/check/mod.rs:2063-2162`. `op.is_arithmetic()` branch at `2138-2159` requires `Type::Int | Type::Int64` on the left, fixes the width, and calls `expect_int_width`. **For Float this guard must be widened to include `Type::Float`.** Note the Vec-refusal at `2072-2077` (`op != Add`).\n- **`expect_int_width`** — `src/check/mod.rs:2044-2061`: errors naming `i64(...)` when Int and Int64 meet. Float would need its own \"cannot mix\" handling (or a bridge builtin).\n- **`plus()`** — `src/check/mod.rs:2168-2200`: `+` dispatches on operand type (Int/Int64 → `Arith`; Str → `Concat`; Vec → `Concat`; else error). **Float needs an `Arith` arm added here.**\n- **Comparison operators** — `binary()`'s `op.is_comparison()` branch: `src/check/mod.rs:2079-2136`. For `Int | Int64` it uses `expect_int_width`; otherwise generic `expect` against the left type. **Float comparison needs a decision**: either join the integer-width branch or fall to the generic path (both operands must already agree). The composite/`contains_vec` refusals are at `2085-2090`.\n- **Index uses `Int`** — `src/check/mod.rs:1959`: `expect(ctx, index, &Type::Int)`. (Float indices presumably stay Int.)\n- Builtin signatures — **`builtin()`** — `src/check/mod.rs:414-450`: `str`→`Int` (`417-423`), `range`→`Int` (`424-430`), `i64`→`Int`→`Int64` (`441-447`). If you add a `str`-for-Float or a Float bridge, this is where.\n- `BUILTIN_NAMES` — `src/check/mod.rs:395-410` (docs.rs reads this list).\n- `orderable()` — `src/check/mod.rs:1810-1812` restricts `sort` to `Int | Int64 | Str | Char`; `sort_call` at `1818-1844`. Decide whether Float joins `sort`'s ordering domain.\n- **Input-type refusals** — `input_read` (`2442-2489`) and `inputs_read` (`2494-2543`) call `want.contains_int64()` (`2469-2476`, `2526-2533`). **Float is the opposite of Int64 here**: a JSON number is exactly a double, so you may *allow* Float input where Int64 is refused — a design decision you'll make at these two sites.\n- `unify` (generic matching) — `src/check/mod.rs:637-665` — scalar arm falls to `template == concrete`, so a new scalar needs no change there.\n\n---\n\n## 5. `src/check/linearity.rs` — does `Int` appear?\n\nYes, only as a leaf-kind in the walkers (a Float literal would be a leaf too):\n- `Kind::Int(_)` in `stream_uses` leaf arm — `src/check/linearity.rs:40`.\n- `Kind::Int(_)` in `any_node` leaf arm — `src/check/linearity.rs:169`.\n- `Kind::Int(_)` in `calls_in` leaf arm — `src/check/linearity.rs:256`.\n\nNothing type-specific; just add `Kind::Float` to the same leaf arms.\n\n---\n\n## 6. `src/tags.rs` — node-type tags\n\n- `walk()` leaf arm lists `Kind::Int(_)` — `src/tags.rs:34`.\n- **`tag()`** — `src/tags.rs:107-143`; the literal tag is at **`110-113`**: `Kind::Int(_) if tir.ty == Type::Int64 => \"int64\"`, else `\"int\"`. \"One literal kind, two widths: which one this literal has is the node's type's to say.\" For Float (one width) a single `\"float\"` tag suffices.\n- `binop_tag()` maps ops to `arith.add/sub/mul/div/rem` and `compare.eq/ne/lt/le/gt/ge` — `src/tags.rs:145-159`.\n- `builtin_tag()` — `src/tags.rs:161-175` (`IntToStr`→`\"str\"`, `IntToI64`→`\"i64\"`).\n\n`tests/tag_corpus.rs` rewrites every corpus case's `node_types:` line on each test run.\n\n---\n\n## 7. `src/emit_*.rs` — each backend (Int literal / arithmetic / comparison / printer)\n\n### `emit_go.rs`\n- **Literal**: `Kind::Int(n) => int_lit(&t.ty, *n)` — `878`; `int_lit()` — `1255-1261`: `tlInt64(n)` for Int64 else `tlInt(n)`. Helpers: `INT_HELPER` `tlInt(n int32) int32` — `167-168`; `INT64_HELPER` `tlInt64(n int64) int64` — `172-173` (both defeat Go's exact constant folding).\n- **Arithmetic**: `Kind::Arith` — `928` → `arith()` — `1267-1287`: Int64 uses `tlDiv64/tlRem64`; Int uses `tlDiv/tlRem`; `+ - *` are native. `ARITH_HELPER` (`tlDiv/tlRem`, int32) — `119-132`; `ARITH64_HELPER` — `136-149`.\n- **Comparison**: `Kind::Compare` — `991` → `compare()` — `1136-1145`; `go_op()` maps ops — `1289-1299`. Composite equality via `tlEq` (`EQ_HELPER` — `155-156`).\n- **Printer**: `show()` — `1147-1199`; **`Type::Int` → `strconv.FormatInt(int64({value}), 10)`** at `1155`; `Type::Int64` at `1156`. `show_fn` — `1249-1251`.\n- **Type mapping**: `go_type()` — `670-701`: `Type::Int`→`\"int32\"` (`674`), `Int64`→`\"int64\"` (`676`). `has_scalar` — `473-...`: `Int|Int64|Bool` count as scalars for the `strconv` import (`483`).\n- **Helper selection**: reads emitted text — `392-435` (`arith`/`arith64` at `394-395`; helper table `408-430`); `itoa` usage tracked in walk at `627`/`636`.\n- `walk()` leaf arm `Kind::Int` — `563`.\n\nFor Float (Go `float64`): add a `tlFloat`-style literal (Go constant-folds float arithmetic too, so an escape like `tlInt` is likely needed), `arith` arms, a `strconv.FormatFloat` printer branch, and `go_type` → `\"float64\"`.\n\n### `emit_jq.rs`\n- **Literal**: `Kind::Int(n) => n.to_string()` — `462`. jq has one number type; Int64 literal also prints via this (both widths are doubles).\n- **Arithmetic**: `Kind::Arith` — `500` → `arith()` — `710-730`: Int64 uses `tl_div64/tl_rem64` (helper `52-56`), `+ - *` native on doubles; Int uses `tl_div/tl_rem/tl_mul` + `tl_i32` wrap (helper `28-43`). `uses_arith` decides which helper block — `350-...` (checks `t.ty == Type::Int64` at `354`).\n- **Comparison**: `Kind::Compare` — `559-561` → `jq_op()` — `732-742`.\n- **Printer**: `canonical()` — `143-...`; **`Type::Str | Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Bool => value.to_string()`** at `150`.\n\nFor Float: since jq computes in doubles, Float is close to free here (a literal is `n.to_string()`); decide whether Float joins the arith helpers or uses native jq arithmetic. Watch the `canonical` printer arm.\n\n### `emit_js.rs`\n- **Literal**: `Kind::Int(n) => int_lit(&t.ty, *n)` — `595`; `int_lit()` — `843-849`: Int64 → `\"{n}n\"` (BigInt), Int → `n.to_string()`.\n- **Arithmetic**: `Kind::Arith` — `630` → `arith()` — `854-876`: Int64 uses `tl_div64/tl_rem64` + `BigInt.asIntN(64, ...)`; Int uses `tl_div/tl_rem`, `Math.imul`, `(x|0)`. Helpers `ARITH_HELPER` (`53-63`), `ARITH64_HELPER` (`68-76`).\n- **Comparison**: `Kind::Compare` — `708` → `compare()` — `454-471`; `compare_helpers()` — `441-445` (Str codepoint order via `tl_str_cmp`, composite equality via `tl_eq`); `js_op()` — `878-888`. Int/Int64 comparisons are native `===`/`<` etc.\n- **Printer**: `show()` — `306-349`; **`Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Bool => String({value})`** at `315`.\n- `used_helpers` walk leaf `Kind::Int` — `485`.\n\nFor Float (JS `Number` = binary64): Float is *exactly* JS's native number, so a literal is `n.to_string()`, arithmetic is native, and the printer is `String(...)`. The one subtlety: an Int64 is a BigInt, so mixing Float (Number) with Int64 in one program has no JS-native operator — your type rules must keep them apart (they can't mix anyway per the checker).\n\n### `emit_lua.rs`\n- **Literal**: `Kind::Int(n) => n.to_string()` — `678`.\n- **Arithmetic**: `Kind::Arith` — `714` → `arith()` — `898-918`: Int64 uses `tl_div64/tl_rem64` (Lua ints are 64-bit); Int uses `tl_div/tl_rem` + `tl_i32` wrap. Helpers `ARITH_HELPER` (`145-161`), `ARITH64_HELPER` (`168-176`). Helper selection at `629-637` (`used.arith64`/`used.arith`).\n- **Comparison**: `Kind::Compare` — `766` → `compare()` — `513-522`; `compare_helpers()` — `537-539`; `lua_op()` — `920-930`.\n- **Printer**: `show()` — `355-402`; **`Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Bool => tostring({value})`** at `363`.\n- `used_helpers` walk leaf `Kind::Int` — `556`.\n\nFor Float (Lua `number`): Lua 5.3+ has integer vs float subtypes; `tostring` on a float gives the right decimal. Decide whether Float is a Lua float and whether arithmetic needs care.\n\n### `emit_py.rs`\n- **Literal**: `Kind::Int(n) => n.to_string()` — `366`.\n- **Arithmetic**: `Kind::Arith` — `401` → `arith()` — `603-623`: Int64 via `tl_i64` wrap (`I64_HELPER` `37-39`); Int via `tl_i32` (`I32_HELPER` `31-33`); div/rem via `tl_div64/tl_rem64`/`tl_div/tl_rem` (`ARITH_HELPER` `43-55`, `ARITH64_HELPER` `59-71`). Helper selection — `209-247` (`arith`/`arith64` at `211-212`).\n- **Comparison**: `Kind::Compare` — `451-453` → `py_op()` — `625-635`. Native `<` etc. (Python ints arbitrary precision).\n- **Printer**: `show()` — `307-353`; **`Type::Int | Type::Int64 => str({value})`** at `315`.\n\nFor Float (Python `float`): literal is `n.to_string()`; decide whether Float uses native Python `+ - * / %` (Python `%` on floats works) or a helper; printer `str(...)`.\n\n### `emit_rs.rs`\n- **Literal**: `Kind::Int(n) => int_lit(&t.ty, *n)` — `1053`; `int_lit()` — `1380-1386`: Int64 → `\"{n}i64\"`, Int → `tl_int({n})` (`INT_HELPER` `36-39`, defeats const-folding of literal overflow).\n- **Arithmetic**: `Kind::Arith` — `1087` → `arith()` — `1399-1410`: `wrapping_add/sub/mul`; div/rem via `tl_div64/tl_rem64` (Int64, `ARITH64_HELPER` `58-71`) or `tl_div/tl_rem` (`ARITH_HELPER` `41-54`). Helper selection — `562-604`.\n- **Comparison**: `Kind::Compare` — `1137-1139` → `rs_op()` — `1412-1422`. Native `==`/`<` etc.\n- **Printer**: `show()` — `1282-1331`; **`Type::Int | Type::Int64 => ({value}).to_string()`** at `1289`.\n- **Type mapping**: `rs_type()` — `804-826`: `Int`→`\"i32\"` (`808`), `Int64`→`\"i64\"` (`809`).\n- **Input parsing**: `parser_expr()` — `831-850`: `Int`→`tl_parse_i32` (`835`); `Int64`→`unreachable!` (input barred, `837`). `tl_parse_i32` — `312-330` (rejects a non-integer at `324-326`). `descriptor`-style input isn't in Rust (it has its own parser).\n- `walk`/used leaf `Kind::Int` — `702`.\n\nFor Float (Rust `f64`): literal `n.to_string()` (or `{n}f64`), native `+ - *` (no wrapping), `tl_div`/`tl_rem` need float variants (or rely on Rust's `/` and `%` for f64, which don't panic but `/0.0` → inf/NaN — decide semantics), printer `.to_string()`, `rs_type` → `\"f64\"`, and a Float parser (`tl_parse_f64`).\n\n### `emit_toylang.rs` (formatter / source re-printer)\n- This works on the *source AST* (`Expr`), not TIR, so it has no `Int64`/width concept.\n- `Expr::Int { value, .. } => value.to_string()` — `src/emit_toylang.rs:407`; `Expr::Neg` printing — `428` and `627`.\n- `bin_power()` — `55-61` (only BinOp precedence; no type involvement).\n\nFor Float: add an `Expr::Float` printing arm. Since the formatter re-derives precedence from `BinOp`, arithmetic needs no change here.\n\n---\n\n## 8. `src/emit_llvm.rs` — native backend specifics\n\n- **`RUNTIME_C`** — `src/emit_llvm.rs:34`: `pub const RUNTIME_C: &str = include_str!(\"../runtime/toylang.c\");`. Written and linked in `lib.rs:340-353`.\n- **Int representation**: **every scalar lives in an `i64` slot** — `llvm_type()` at `304-325`: `Type::Int => i64_type()` (`311`) and `Type::Int64 => i64_type()` (`315`). Doc at `312-314`: \"an Int is computed wide and narrowed after every operation; an Int64 is that representation with the narrowing left off.\" Float would be an `f64` (LLVM `f64_type()`), so its `llvm_type` is the one scalar that differs from the `i64` pattern.\n- **Literal**: `Kind::Int(n) => i64 const` — `src/emit_llvm.rs:1217`.\n- **Arithmetic**: `Kind::Arith` — `1405` → `arith_at()` — `1781-1793` dispatches to `arith()` (32-bit, `1758-1777`, computes wide in i64 then `narrow_to_i32` at `1839-1847`) or `arith64()` (native i64 wrapping, `1803-1836`, div/rem via i128). `trap_if_zero` — `1849-1880`.\n- **Comparison**: `compare()` — `2212-2272`; int predicate table (EQ/NE/SLT/SLE/SGT/SGE) — `2231-2239`; Int/Int64/Bool/Char are compared as i64 directly — `2261-2263`. `equal()` (structural/composite) — `1890-1938`.\n- **Printer**: `show()` — `937-...`; **`Type::Int | Type::Int64 => call tl_int_to_str`** at `950` (doc: \"already takes the full i64, so both widths print through it\"). Float would need a new runtime `tl_float_to_str` (or format through `snprintf %g`).\n- **Slot conversion**: `to_slot()`/`read_slot()` — `410-444`: `Int|Int64|Char` pass through as the i64 slot (`415`, `432`). A Float is an f64, so the slot model (raw 8-byte `int64_t` column) needs a decision: a Float won't bit-fit the int64 slot cleanly unless you reuse it by bit-cast (which is exactly what an f64 in an 8-byte slot is).\n- **Input descriptor**: `descriptor()` — `2426-...`: `Type::Int => \"i\"` (`2438`), `Int64 => unreachable!` (barred, `2440`). The C parser reads these — `runtime/toylang.c:747-770` (the `'i'` case, which rejects `.`/`e` at `758-761`, the \"float where Int was declared is an error\" path). A Float input type needs a new descriptor char (e.g. `\"f\"`) and a C `case` that calls `strtod` and accepts exponents.\n- **Runtime C int printing**: `tl_int_to_str(int64_t)` — `runtime/toylang.c:51-58`; `tl_print` — `82-87`.\n- `rt` struct bindings (runtime function declarations) — `src/emit_llvm.rs:120-278` (`int_to_str` at `128-133`). Float builtins/formatting would be declared here.\n\n---\n\n## 9. `src/prelude.rs` — does `Int` appear?\n\n**No.** `prelude.rs` only defines `checked()` from `prelude.toy` and `inject()`. `prelude.toy` (the source) declares `Opt<T>`, `Result<T, E>`, `join_lines`, `join` — **no Int**. Nothing Float-specific to do here unless you add prelude functions. (`build.rs` generates `prelude_checked.rs` from this source; it has `Type::Int`/`Int64` and `Kind::Int` codegen arms at `build.rs:144-145` and `build.rs:362` — add a Float arm there if prelude ever uses a float literal.)\n\n---\n\n## 10. `src/tir.rs` fusion — does `Int` appear?\n\nFusion (`flatten`, `tir.rs:326-389`) only matches stream shapes (`Inputs`, `Lines`, `Var`, `Local`, `Bind`, `Map`, `Select`, `Call`); `Kind::Int` falls into the `_ => unreachable!` arm — no change needed for Float. `runtime_elem`/`vec_depth` (`tir.rs:267-284`) are type-generic.\n\n---\n\n## 11. `src/input.rs` — input validation of `Int`\n\n- **`validate()`** — `src/input.rs:11-88`. The `(Type::Int, Value::Number(n))` arm — `15-31`: requires `n.as_i64()`, then enforces the 32-bit rule via `i32::try_from`. A float in the JSON (`36.5`) fails `as_i64()` → \"expected Int, found the non-integer number\" (`19-23`). **This is the \"float where Int was declared is an error\" rule.** For a `Type::Float`, you'd add a `(Type::Float, Value::Number(n))` arm accepting any JSON number (via `as_f64`).\n- `describe()` — `90-99` (falls back to \"a number\" for non-matching numbers).\n- `to_lua()` — `101-124`: converts JSON numbers to Lua integer or float — already handles both (`105-107`).\n\n---\n\n## 12. Tests\n\n**Corpus cases** — `tests/corpus/`. Representative Int arithmetic cases:\n- `arith_add.yaml` (`tests/corpus/arith_add.yaml`): `str(1 + 2)` → `3`, with `node_types: [arith.add, builtin.str, int]`. This is the canonical Int-arithmetic case.\n- Also: `arith_sub.yaml`, `arith_mul.yaml`, `arith_div_trunc.yaml`, `arith_rem_sign.yaml`, `arith_precedence.yaml`, `arith_neg.yaml`, `div_by_zero.yaml`, `int_result.yaml`, `wrap_add.yaml`, `wrap_div_min.yaml`.\n- **Int64 corpus cases** (the template for a numeric-type feature's happy paths): `int64_compare.yaml`, `int64_composite_equality.yaml`, `int64_div_rem.yaml`, `int64_in_containers.yaml`, `int64_literal_by_position.yaml`, `int64_negative_literal.yaml`, `int64_record_beside_input.yaml`, `int64_widening.yaml`, `sort_int64s.yaml`.\n\n**How cases run across backends** — `tests/corpus.rs` + `tests/support/mod.rs`:\n- `corpus.rs:13-40` (`every_backend_agrees_and_is_right`) runs every case through `support::agreement_failures`.\n- `corpus.rs:45-62` (`emitted_code_matches_the_snapshot`) snapshots emitted code for cases that ask (`snapshot:` list).\n- `support/mod.rs`: `Fields` struct with `deny_unknown_fields` (`20-42`); `agreement_failures()` (`65-120`) runs on `Backend::ALL` and flags `RAN`/`BROKEN`/`DISAGREE`/`WRONG`; `cases()` (`126-169`) loads and validates each YAML (exactly one of `output`/`refuses`).\n- `tests/tag_corpus.rs` — regenerates `node_types:` on every run (derived from `tir::Kind` via `tags.rs`).\n\n**Error snapshot tests** — `tests/step_1.rs` through `step_5.rs` (not one file):\n- `step_1.rs:1-3` shows the pattern: `err(src)` = `compile(src).map(|_| ()).unwrap_err().to_string()`, pinned with `insta::assert_snapshot!`. Type-error tests live across these files; e.g. `step_3.rs:59`, `step_4.rs:38`, `step_5.rs:78`.\n- The Int64 type-error snapshots are in `tests/int64.rs` (see below) and `tests/snapshots/int64__*.snap` plus `tests/snapshots/unwrap__an_int_literal_has_to_fit_in_an_int.snap`.\n\n**`tests/int64.rs`** — the model for a new numeric type's dedicated test file (`tests/int64.rs:1-145`). It covers: wrap at 2^63 (`15-29`), `MIN/-1` wrap (`33-54`), jq's documented inexactness (`59-64`), no implicit widening for arithmetic/comparison (`68-86`), too-big literal needs an expected position (`90-97`), `i64` takes an Int (`101-109`), input can't be Int64 (`113-133`), `str` doesn't take Int64 (`137-144`). Each pins a checker error with `insta::assert_snapshot!`. A `tests/float.rs` would mirror this.\n\n**Other test touchpoints for Int**:\n- `tests/step_5.rs:120-127` (`input_float_where_int_declared`) — pins the current \"float where Int declared is an error\" message; this test's meaning changes when Float exists.\n- `tests/type_flow.rs` — many `Type::Int` assertions (e.g. `19-20`, `86`, `95-96`) that construct expected `Type`s; a Float type flow would be tested here.\n- `tests/euler_real_data.rs:25-31` — a real program using both `Int` and `Int64` (good example of a mixed-width program).\n- `tests/backend_go.rs:48` — asserts `strconv` import appears when printing an Int (a Float printer will need a similar import assertion, `strconv.FormatFloat`).\n\n---\n\n## Extra touchpoints not in your list (worth knowing)\n\n- **`src/ty.rs`** (the `Type` enum lives here, not just check/types.rs):\n  - `Type::Int` / `Type::Int64` declarations — `src/ty.rs:16-25`.\n  - `Type::from_name` — `95-104` (add `\"Float\"`).\n  - `ident()` (backend type-name fragments) — `236-263` (`Type::Int`→`\"Int\"` at `239`, `Int64`→`\"Int64\"` at `240`).\n  - `Display` — `272-299` (`277-278`).\n  - `PartialEq` — `313-336` (scalar arm `315-319`).\n  - `substitute` — `348-376` (scalar arm `374`).\n  - The `contains_*` predicates (`contains_int64` `171-184`, `contains_opt`, `contains_char`, `contains_vec`) are the pattern for any `contains_float` if you need a containment check.\n- **`src/build.rs`** — `Type::Int`/`Int64` codegen arms `144-145`, `Kind::Int` arm `362`, `Builtin::IntToStr/IntToI64` `171-172` (only matters if the prelude or a builtin uses a Float literal/builtin).\n- **Docs** (mirroring a numeric-type addition): `docs/reference/types/int.md`, `docs/reference/types/int64.md`, `docs/adr/0010-int64-is-a-second-integer-type.md`, `docs/reference/builtins/i64.md`, `docs/reference/operators/arithmetic.md`. `tests/docs.rs:130-145` enforces that every *builtin* has a reference page (types are not enforced). A Float would warrant an analogous `docs/reference/types/float.md` and possibly a new ADR.\n\n---\n\n### The concrete mirror checklist (condensed)\n\nTo add `Float`, the sites you must touch, by file:\n\n| File | What |\n|---|---|\n| `src/ast.rs` | `Expr::Float` variant + `span()` arm; decide Float in `BinOp` semantics |\n| `src/parse.rs` | new `Tok::Float`, lexer arm (digits + `.`/exp), `atom()` arm |\n| `src/ty.rs` | `Type::Float`; `from_name`; `ident`; `Display`; `PartialEq`; `substitute`; any `contains_*` |\n| `src/tir.rs` | `Kind::Float` variant; `each_node` leaf arm |\n| `src/check/mod.rs` | literal synth; `binary()` arithmetic guard; `plus()`; comparison branch; `orderable`/`sort`; input-refusal decisions; (optional) Float builtin/bridge |\n| `src/check/types.rs` | nothing (via `from_name`) unless arity rules differ |\n| `src/check/linearity.rs` | leaf arms (3 places) |\n| `src/tags.rs` | `Kind::Float` leaf + `tag()`; optional `builtin_tag` |\n| each `src/emit_*.rs` | literal emission, `arith`, comparison, printer, type mapping, helper/import selection, walk leaf arms |\n| `src/emit_llvm.rs` | `llvm_type` (f64), literal, `arith_at` (float ops), `compare`, `show` (new `tl_float_to_str`), `to_slot`/`read_slot` (f64 vs i64 slot), `descriptor` (`\"f\"` + C parser case) |\n| `runtime/toylang.c` | new float print/parse/arith functions, descriptor case |\n| `src/input.rs` | `(Type::Float, Number)` validate arm |\n| `src/build.rs` | only if prelude/builtins gain float use |\n| `tests/` | `float.rs` mirroring `int64.rs`; corpus cases (`arith_add` model); `step_5.rs:122` float-input test; `type_flow.rs`; `backend_go.rs` import test |\n| `docs/` | `types/float.md`, possibly an ADR, operators page |\n\nThe single most important structural decision: **whether Float's literal is typed by position like Int/Int64** (it's a single width, so probably just always `Type::Float`), and **whether Float arithmetic wraps** (it can't — decide NaN/overflow semantics, and whether `0.0` division traps like the integer `tl_div` does). That decision ripples through every `arith` helper in §7.\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_fa1260973ffeOIaNGhvLCc80qv","sessionId":"ses_fa121c1e5ffeGR4RxLtHYkn0VL","model":{"modelID":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731","providerID":"openrouter"},"truncated":false},"title":"Explore Int handling across backends","time":{"start":1788298018332,"end":1788298486291}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_05ede13c2001aQmWg5E5i7NGNi","sessionID":"ses_fa1260973ffeOIaNGhvLCc80qv","messageID":"msg_05ede0bde001luxys8I2pybJsh"}}
