{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788298595713,"sessionID":"ses_fa1260973ffeOIaNGhvLCc80qv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a385d9409006d225","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"which node go jq lua python3 rustc cc 2>&1"},"output":"/home/kantord/.nvm/versions/node/v24.7.0/bin/node\n/usr/bin/go\n/usr/bin/jq\n/usr/bin/lua\n/home/kantord/.pyenv/shims/python3\n/usr/bin/rustc\n/usr/bin/cc\n","metadata":{"output":"/home/kantord/.nvm/versions/node/v24.7.0/bin/node\n/usr/bin/go\n/usr/bin/jq\n/usr/bin/lua\n/home/kantord/.pyenv/shims/python3\n/usr/bin/rustc\n/usr/bin/cc\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"which node go jq lua python3 rustc cc 2>&1","time":{"start":1788298595710,"end":1788298595712}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Excellent, comprehensive report. Now let me make decisions and plan the implementation. This is a large task. Let me think about the key design decisions.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n1. **Float literal**: add `Expr::Float { value: f64 }` in AST, `Tok::Float(f64)` token, `Kind::Float(f64)` in TIR. A float literal is always `Type::Float` (single width, no position-based resolution needed).\n\n2. **Float printing format**: This is the \"needs a call\" item. I need to decide a format all 7 backends produce byte-for-byte. Let me think carefully.\n\nThe cleanest implementable decision: Since the ADR says \"Float is JavaScript's number\", and JS is the one backend where formatting is free (`String(n)`), I'll specify **ECMAScript Number::toString**. But implementing it in C/Lua/Go/Python/jq/Rust is the hard part.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Let me think about what the existing backends already produce natively for a double and find a common normalization.\n\nLet me reconsider the practical set of values and whether I can normalize cheaply.\n\nActually, I realize the smartest approach given the constraints: I'll implement a **shared formatting helper per backend** that takes the double and produces the canonical string. The canonical format I'll pick must be easy to produce in all 7.\n\nLet me reconsider using each backend's shortest-roundtrip + normalize. But the exponent-handling is the issue.\n\nAlternative clean decision: **Always print in decimal (never scientific) with the shortest round-trip digits**, and for the integral case append \".0\"? No wait, that fails for large/small values where native formatting switches to scientific (JS/Go/Python/C switch to exponent for 1e21).\n\nHmm. But how likely are the corpus tests to hit 1e21? The acceptance criteria tests: literals, arithmetic, NaN/Infinity, div-by-zero. If I write corpus cases with \"nice\" values, I control what's tested. But a robust implementation should handle all values. The issue says \"exact float literal printing so all seven backends agree byte for byte\". \n\nI think I need to actually decide and implement a real format. Let me go with ECMAScript Number::toString, because:\n1. It's the principled choice given the ADR.\n2. It's well-specified.\n3. JS backend is free.\n\nFor the other 6, I implement it. Let me think about how to get shortest-roundtrip digits + EC formatting in each:\n\nThe core challenge is shortest-roundtrip. Let me reconsider: do I actually need shortest-roundtrip, or can I use a fixed-precision approach that round-trips and agrees?\n\nActually, here's a cleaner idea that avoids implementing Ryu everywhere: **Use a fixed, high-precision representation and a canonical algorithm.** No — fixed precision won't round-trip for all and won't be \"shortest\".\n\nLet me reconsider the actual requirement. The corpus compares output bytes. For arithmetic results (like 1/3 = 0.3333333333333333), all backends using shortest-roundtrip will produce \"0.3333333333333333\". So shortest-roundtrip gives agreement naturally for these. The format differences are only:\n1. Integral values: \"1\" vs \"1.0\"\n2. Exponent notation: \"1e+21\" vs \"1e+21\" vs \"1e+21\" vs \"1000000000000000000000\"\n\nSo the realistic implementation: use each backend's shortest-roundtrip formatting to get digits, then normalize:\n- If the string contains 'e'/'E' → normalize exponent form to EC style\n- Else if it's an integral string (no '.') → decide integral representation\n\nGiven the difficulty of normalizing exponent forms across languages (Go uses \"1e+21\", Python \"1e+21\", Lua \"1e+21\", JS \"1e+21\", C \"%g\" gives \"1e+21\" with %.17g... but shortest needs %.17g then trim), let me think about whether I can avoid scientific entirely.\n\nActually, the simplest robust decision that avoids ALL scientific-notation normalization: **Never use scientific notation; always print the full decimal.** But JS/Go/C/Python/Lua all switch to scientific at 1e21 automatically, so I'd have to defeat that. In JS I could use `toFixed` with enough digits then trim — but `toFixed(20)` loses precision beyond 20 digits... For 1e21 the integer needs 22 digits. Hmm.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually — let me reconsider the whole scope. Given that this is a huge task and the printing format is explicitly \"not yet settled, needs a call\", maybe I should ESCALATE the printing-format decision per the brief: \"If something needs deciding that this brief does not settle, write ESCALATION.md... take the most conservative continuation.\"\n\nBut the brief also says \"take the most conservative continuation, and keep going; never stop to wait for a human.\" So I'd write ESCALATION.md documenting the decision, pick the most conservative option, commit it, and continue. The most conservative option = the one requiring least code and least risk = use each backend's native shortest-roundtrip and normalize the integral case.\n\nHmm, but actually I want to make real progress. Let me think about what decision lets me actually implement all 7 backends with reasonable code.\n\nLet me reconsider. What do the 7 backends natively produce for a double, using their shortest-roundtrip, for a set of representative values?\n\nLet me define the DECISION as: **A Float prints as the shortest round-trip decimal, with integral values printed without a decimal point, and using scientific notation (with a specific exponent format) when the native shortest-roundtrip representation uses it.** \n\nBut \"when native uses it\" differs. I need ONE rule.\n\nOK let me just pick the ECMAScript rule and implement it. To get shortest-roundtrip digits, the key realization: **each backend's native `tostring`/`repr`/`format` of a double already produces the shortest round-trip decimal digits** (all modern implementations use Grisu/Ryu): Rust `{}`, Python `repr`, Go `strconv.FormatFloat('g', -1)`, JS `String(n)`, Lua `tostring`, jq `tostring`. C — this is the only one without it natively; I'll implement shortest-roundtrip in the LLVM runtime.\n\nThen I reformat each backend's native output to EC style. But the reformatting (parsing \"1e+21\" and \"1.23e-7\" into EC style) is itself per-language work and error-prone.\n\nThis is a LOT. Let me reconsider whether there's a dramatically simpler path.\n\n**Simpler decision: use the ECMAScript format, but get it by having every backend format through a SINGLE shared routine.** That's not possible across 7 languages.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider the integral case and just go with matching **Rust's f64 Display** as the spec. Why? Because:\n1. Rust is the implementation language — the \"reference\" backend.\n2. Rust's `{}` is shortest-roundtrip and well-defined.\n3. I can test the Rust backend's output against the Rust host's own formatting trivially.\n\nRust f64 Display rules (I should verify): Let me recall precisely. Rust's `{}` for f64 uses the shortest representation. For the decimal/scientific choice, Rust uses: scientific notation when the exponent is < -4 or... let me actually just test this since I have Rust. But I can't easily compile a scratch rust binary without /tmp (denied). Hmm. Actually I can write a small program in the worktree and use `cargo`/`rustc`... but \"direct binary execution such as `cargo run -- run some_file.toy`\" is denied — that's specifically about the toylang binary. Can I run `rustc` on a scratch file? The denial says \"direct binary execution such as cargo run -- run some_file.toy or ./target/debug/toylang\". That's about the toylang tool. Running `python3 script.py` is explicitly allowed. Running a scratch rust compile... probably allowed since it's not the toylang binary, but let me avoid it and rely on knowledge.\n\nActually, I can use Python to investigate formatting behavior since Python's float repr is also shortest-roundtrip and I know it well. But Rust differs from Python on exponent thresholds.\n\nLet me recall Rust f64 `{}` formatting precisely:\n- Rust uses the algorithm that produces shortest digits.\n- Format: if the value is integral and fits... actually Rust prints `1.0` as \"1\", `100.0` as \"100\".\n- Rust switches to scientific when... I recall: `format!(\"{}\", 1e20)` = \"100000000000000000000\", `format!(\"{}\", 1e21)` = \"1e21\". And `format!(\"{}\", 1e-5)` = \"0.00001\", `format!(\"{}\", 1e-6)` = \"0.000001\"? Actually I think Rust switches to scientific below 1e-5 or so. Let me recall the actual rule.\n\nRust's `float_to_general_debug`... The Display implementation uses `dtoa`/`ryu`. The rule for choosing decimal vs scientific in Rust Display: it's based on the decimal exponent. I believe Rust uses scientific when exponent < -5 or exponent >= 16? No...\n\nThis uncertainty is a problem. I don't want to spec a format I can't pin down.\n\nGiven the uncertainty, let me go with **ECMAScript Number::toString** because it's a precise, published spec and JS gives it for free. I'll implement it in the other backends. To avoid implementing Ryu in C, I can have the C/LLVM runtime produce shortest-roundtrip via a known technique, OR — key insight — I can have the toylang compiler (Rust) itself do the formatting? No, formatting happens at runtime in emitted code.\n\nWait — actually, here's a much better idea for the C/LLVM backend. I can implement the EC format in C using `snprintf(\"%.17g\")` — no. Let me just implement shortest-roundtrip in C via the classic \"print with %.17e then find shortest that round-trips\" approach:\n\nActually, the standard approach for shortest round-trip in C without Ryu:\n```\nfor digits from 1 to 17:\n    snprintf(buf, \"%.{digits}e\", value);\n    if strtod(buf) == value: break;  // first digits that round-trips = shortest\n```\nThis is O(17) per print but correct and simple! This finds the shortest mantissa that round-trips. This is a well-known technique (David Gay / others). It's not as fast as Ryu but correctness matters more. This works in C for the LLVM runtime.\n\nFor the other backends, I can use the SAME technique: iteratively format with increasing precision and check round-trip, or use their native shortest. Actually, using the iterative technique in each backend is uniform and avoids relying on each language's exact native format. But it's more code in each language.\n\nHmm, but the iterative technique still needs to then format into EC style (decimal vs scientific, integral handling). That's the same normalization everywhere.\n\nLet me simplify the DECISION to make implementation uniform:\n\n**DECISION (Float printing):** A Float prints exactly per ECMAScript's `Number.prototype.toString` (ECMA-262 Number::toString). Concretely:\n- NaN → \"NaN\", +∞ → \"Infinity\", -∞ → \"-Infinity\", ±0 → \"0\"\n- Otherwise, let s be the shortest decimal such that s round-trips. Let n be its exponent (position of decimal point), k = number of significant digits.\n- If k ≤ n ≤ 21: print the integer digits followed by (n−k) zeros (integer form, no decimal point).\n- If 0 < n ≤ 21: insert the decimal point after n digits.\n- If −6 < n ≤ 0: \"0.\" + (−n zeros) + digits.\n- Otherwise: scientific, \"d[.ddd]e±E\" where E = n−1 (no leading zeros in E, '+' for non-negative E).\n\nTo get shortest digits in each backend, use the backend's shortest-roundtrip native formatting, then normalize. But to avoid per-language parse-of-exponent complexity, I'll implement the iterative approach where needed.\n\nActually, this is a huge amount of careful per-language code. Let me reconsider the effort/benefit and whether there's an even simpler decision that the corpus tests will actually exercise.\n\nGiven the acceptance criteria and realistic tests, the values exercised will be things like: 3.14, 0.5, 1.5, 2.0, 0.1, 0.3333333333333333 (1/3), NaN, Infinity, -Infinity, 0.0, 1.0, maybe 2.5. All of these have simple shortest-roundtrip digits with NO scientific notation. The integral ones (2.0, 1.0, 0.0) are the only formatting-divergence point (\"2\" vs \"2.0\").\n\nSo the MINIMUM to make realistic tests pass: normalize the integral case (append or strip \".0\"). The scientific case (1e21) is edge behavior.\n\nBut I want to be robust. Let me pick a decision that's both principled and implementable with bounded effort:\n\n**FINAL DECISION: ECMAScript Number::toString.** I'll implement it in each backend. For shortest-roundtrip digits I'll use each backend's native shortest formatting and normalize. I'll handle the integral and scientific cases.\n\nHmm, but normalizing scientific from native forms across 6 languages is genuinely a lot of fiddly code, and getting it wrong means byte mismatch. Let me reconsider one more time.\n\nActually, you know what, let me reconsider using the native output of each language but choosing the decision to be exactly \"**match JavaScript's String()**\" and verify with a quick Python investigation of what JS/Go/etc produce... I can't easily run JS/Go here? Actually I probably can (node, go may be installed). Let me check what tools are available.\n\nActually the cleanest thing: let me just test what each language produces natively for a set of doubles, using scripts I write and run (python3 allowed; and I could write small .js/.go/.lua/.py and run node/go/lua/python3 — running those interpreters on scratch files isn't the denied \"run the toylang binary\"). Let me check tool availability.\n\nLet me check which interpreters exist.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05ee70c30001VMuuGSWt3o7PCq","sessionID":"ses_fa1260973ffeOIaNGhvLCc80qv","messageID":"msg_05ee5622e001orcI3cQstWoX1R"}}
